#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af01bddd-bdb8-54cf-8470-109a4099c778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π\\sqrt{2} + 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173747} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a1fddb-d216-5be1-a2d7-65f4e0e9836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π + \\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173748} {:answer/id #uuid "5c21a5d5-0eb5-520d-8def-2a8119579b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π(5\\sqrt{5} - 1))/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173749} {:answer/id #uuid "60dd3b59-c02c-5d61-bc38-c0dba532a31e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π\\sqrt{2} + 3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173750} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc75ec6-89f5-59ba-b3dd-dc265bdd2826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π(3\\sqrt{3} - 2))/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173751}] :answer-field/id #uuid "010dbb7c-fb65-531f-9b56-64aba3661590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173746}] :question/id #uuid "29d4c247-c6fe-5732-b0d8-fc8ae1646f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109763" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint $x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176065 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c8e0a-12da-57b1-b178-119e2ed0094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415889} {:answer/id #uuid "134a8ec5-3c99-5a35-b135-9550a1a589e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415890} {:answer/id #uuid "4281a994-2dee-5d00-a661-565bf6f7aacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi (\\sqrt{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415899} {:answer/id #uuid "543c20d4-2012-58fc-988d-4183a7a0d1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415902} {:answer/id #uuid "f67ff004-d369-5080-8ecc-4238d9b7be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543c20d4-2012-58fc-988d-4183a7a0d1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415902} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42dd57a-1d8c-5adc-b40d-c45195678c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "139ae790-9731-55fc-ab5e-266ae6726353" :question/math-academy-id "q-109765" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z=xy$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=xy,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0faa92e0f89c86248abffad7f61cac5d94a3848a7f7be4464c4b28d3915aa32.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(y)^{2}+(x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\sqrt{1+{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}\\,{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}[{2}^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi (2\\sqrt{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f26c9a8-c236-5b49-a03a-a9c4e306b3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276025} {:answer/id #uuid "56116490-cd88-5ee8-914f-70566119e239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4867883-7969-53b3-9ce6-9041f96c5468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ede688-6fec-5d91-b089-01387832a30f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276993} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf48e5d-5a33-563d-899e-f6003b1ae552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4867883-7969-53b3-9ce6-9041f96c5468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276959} :answer-field/id #uuid "85db4747-8718-5aa2-9149-1e2cb6cc044a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "433fa395-a287-58c1-aa07-6bcf4cb6b740" :question/math-academy-id "q-161144" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175996 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f6af28-58a5-5980-8be5-d39c9b4b3514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415894} {:answer/id #uuid "3f58fbfa-2a3c-5f8a-8c15-ac63c162c8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415898} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2e9088-2863-5032-aa2c-88ec65760072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415908} {:answer/id #uuid "b6449307-a0ba-56cc-a5f1-291146f541f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415913} {:answer/id #uuid "e8895199-6569-5bad-a980-9e6b74e4814d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f58fbfa-2a3c-5f8a-8c15-ac63c162c8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415898} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4962c93-3582-5793-94a8-d5e64919a899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0071fbbf-507b-5e86-9e07-511fe6080666" :question/math-academy-id "q-161183" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\sqrt{1+4x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1+4x}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a459a9807befc312528771626dca293a7ebbccd97a2c1e938f47a2d90a36726.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3})=2\\sqrt{x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(2\\sqrt{x})}^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+4x}}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{1+4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x+2{x}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2+8)-0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ccebf2-82ed-5d83-b410-939ba5e5d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275529} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc8786b-2181-586e-88b7-5dec8ae2aecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+{e}^{2z}-1}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275880} {:answer/id #uuid "549bec80-04d3-5b1a-a54f-70468df126a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{4}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276229} {:answer/id #uuid "60d578d1-2b32-5020-837e-daeac36cf440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{2}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276348} {:answer/id #uuid "c5030f28-22fb-5802-acf2-25497ced4080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ccebf2-82ed-5d83-b410-939ba5e5d92a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275529} :answer-field/id #uuid "97154eb2-395c-5491-bba8-688c25fe0e0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e867d73-e633-518f-b45d-26ad639d5221" :question/math-academy-id "q-161230" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.$"}]}
